compact deferred-gates list in the handoff so\012   fresh contexts do not repeat discovery or silently forget the original gate.\012   Resume it when ready; do not replace its criteria with smaller adjacent tests.\012\0125. Before coding, write a Batch plan in the current handoff: one concrete outcome,\012   original parent gate, related child IDs, shared harness/setup, combined checks\012   and explicit session exit criteria. Size this plan for roughly 30 minutes of\012   implementation. Newly discovered children remain steps toward this outcome;\012   completing one child cannot redefine the selected batch as finished.\0126. For integration work, compose the required canonical harness first, including\012   real middleware, transport and source families required by the selected proof.\012   Reuse it across related cases in the same session. If an essential boundary\012   remains prepared, carry that gap as unfinished batch work and address it before\012   final validation when feasible. A narrower prepared test is prerequisite\012   evidence, not a reason to defer the missing integration boundary indefinitely.\012\012Implement and verify:\012\012- Target roughly 30 minutes of implementation per invocation, excluding initial\012  discovery and final verification. This is a planning target, not a hard deadline\012  or a minimum to fill with unnecessary work. Plan several related ready child\012  tasks under one parent gate that share code, context and verification.\012- For larger roadmaps, the recommended total worker-session budget is **1 hour**:\012  discovery, implementation, feature verification and compact handoff all fit\012  inside it. Use that allowance to deliver a meaningful feature or coherent\012  milestone, not isolated fragments followed by another bootstrap. The approximate\012  30-minute implementation target is a planning aid, not a mandatory exit point.\012  Leave time for verification and handoff before the configured timeout; do not\012  assume an extra hour for those steps or wait for forced termination. This is\012  not a minimum duration and does not change the configured runner timeout.\012  Stricter roadmap limits and the actual configured timeout always take precedence.\012  Do not change those settings or add unrelated work just to fill the allowance.\012- Keep the declared batch outcome stable. Revise it only for an evidenced change\012  in requirements/dependencies or a real blocker, and record the reason. Completing\012  a new nested checkbox or one source-family case is not a session exit criterion.\012- A checkbox is a progress unit, not a session boundary. After finishing a child,\012  continue the next related ready child in the same session until the planned\012  batch is complete or the remaining session budget must be reserved for\012  verification and handoff. Finish the current coherent coding unit within that\012  budget; do not stop just because one small checkbox is done or begin an unrelated\012  major gate to fill time. If the feature remains incomplete, record its exact\012  next implementation step and pending verification rather than claiming completion.\012- If a gate is too large, break it into ordered, independently verifiable child\012  checkboxes with stable IDs under the same parent. Complete a coherent group\012  per run. Size and difficulty require decomposition, not deferral. Stop earlier\012  if the parent gate closes, no related authorized work can advance, or a stricter\012  explicit roadmap run limit applies. Leave unfinished children for the handoff.\012- A local prerequisite or prepared component test does not close an end-to-end\012  gate. Keep the parent unchecked until its full stated criteria pass; record\012  prerequisite progress with child checkboxes under that parent. Child completion\012  is measurable progress, not a replacement for the full parent acceptance gate.\012- Add follow-up checkboxes for discoveries, repairs and missing verification only\012  when necessary to satisfy an original requirement. Each must identify its\012  original parent ID, the exact acceptance criterion it serves, a concrete result\012  and verification. Keep scope, permissions and completion standards unchanged.\012  Record unrelated opportunities separately as out of scope; do not add them to\012  the actionable checklist or treat them as completion dependencies.\012- **Implementation before feature verification.** Do not spend an iteration merely\012  rerunning the active feature's acceptance/integration tests. Implement the\012  planned feature/batch first; run its feature-level verification once the batch\012  is believed complete enough to satisfy the selected criteria.\012- A failed feature check is durable evidence, not an invitation to immediately\012  rerun it. Across iterations, do not rerun the same failed feature check until\012  implementation materially changes code/configuration relevant to that failure.\012  Record the failed check and the required implementation change in Current handoff.\012- After a feature check fails, the next useful action is normally implementation\012  or repair. A verification-only next iteration is allowed only when the roadmap\012  task itself is explicitly verification/diagnosis/reproduction work, or when a\012  concrete external/environmental condition changed and rerunning the check is\012  necessary to distinguish that change. Record that exception explicitly.\012- Finish the selected coding batch before validation; do not run the same checks\012  after each child. Validate the completed batch once, collect related failures,\012  finish the repair batch, and rerun only failed or invalidated checks **after a\012  relevant repair**. Reuse passing evidence for unchanged code and environments.\012- Inspect executed, passed, failed and skipped counts. A skipped check is not a\012  pass. Distinguish prepared tests, actual integration and deployed behavior.\012- Use existing authorization for tests and side effects. An unchecked deployment\012  or live-provider gate does not itself authorize publication or spending.\012- Do not use subagents. Use Git only inside the applicable repositories.\012\012Update the tracking file and stop this invocation:\012\012- Maintain - [ ] for incomplete gates and - [x] only for gates whose stated\012  acceptance criteria passed. Preserve IDs, unresolved criteria and dated failures.\012- Keep active progress below 96 KiB, with a hard runner limit of 128 KiB. When Runner context requests compaction, compact before implementation; preserve all gate IDs, criteria, checkbox states, unresolved blockers and current evidence references. Never put removed iteration prose into the source roadmap. If required state cannot fit, report the context blocker rather than deleting requirements.\012- Maintain compact Checked-item evidence only for acceptance gates whose current\012  state matters: ID, implemented/verified/deployed state, a short evidence reference,\012  and last relevant change. Do not accumulate one prose log entry per iteration.\012  If new evidence invalidates a checked item, reopen it with the current reason;\012  the runner-owned archive retains earlier snapshots.\012- Replace Current handoff near the top with: active checkbox, criteria closed this\012  iteration, remaining criteria, blocker/dependency, concise verification results\012  including skips, deferred gates with unblock conditions, and the exact next ready\012  checkbox. Delete superseded handoff prose rather than moving it into a history\012  section. Continue the unfinished active gate unless its documented blocker makes\012  another gate ready.\012- Keep timing and exit information concise in the current handoff: approximate\012  discovery/implementation/verification durations when useful, loaded prompt\012  revision, declared batch outcome and exit reason. Do not create a growing dated\012  iteration-history section; the runner archives each boundary snapshot separately.\012  If exiting early with an unfinished outcome, explain only the current reason.\012- Summarize concrete changes and checks; explain any scope change. If no gate\012  closed, identify the material prerequisite advanced and how it reduces the\012  remaining work. Repeated rediscovery, extra notes or adjacent tests alone\012  are not progress toward the active gate.\012- Treat blockers as **gate-local deferrals**, not as a reason to end the long-running\012  executor. When a gate cannot advance, record its blocker, evidence, unblock\012  condition and a bounded retry trigger, then SKIP it and scan every other unchecked\012  gate for useful authorized implementation, repair, investigation or verification.\012  Do not repeatedly spend fresh sessions on an unchanged blocker or failed check.\012- If Runner context says Blocked recovery mode: YES, the previous session exhausted\012  its ready set. Re-evaluate dependency facts once, then actively seek an alternative:\012  another ready gate, an independent child, preparatory work that reduces a blocker,\012  or a newly feasible verification/repair. Replace stale handoff targets rather than\012  retrying the same stuck action merely because it was previously active.\012- In the bounded `## Deferred gates` section, flag each problematic task using one\012  line: `- BLOCKED TASK-ID: reason | Unblock: exact condition | Retry: trigger`,\012  `- NEEDS_INFO TASK-ID: question | Unblock: required answer | Retry: answer arrives`,\012  `- PROBLEM TASK-ID: failed check | Unblock: repair | Retry: changed evidence`, or\012  `- SKIPPED TASK-ID: dependency | Unblock: prerequisite | Retry: prerequisite done`.\012  Keep these task IDs stable, remove the flag when resolved, and leave the task's\012  acceptance checkbox unchecked until implementation and validation both pass.\012  A deferral is visible unfinished work. Prefer independent ready tasks over a\012  repeated attempt on unchanged blockers; ask one precise question when input is needed.\012- Status: BLOCKED is a **non-terminal recovery signal**. Use it only when this\012  session finds no materially useful authorized action after scanning ALL remaining\012  gates and prerequisites. Record every blocking dependency and exact external\012  unblock action. The runner will start another fresh recovery worker rather than\012  exit. Prefer Status: IN_PROGRESS whenever any useful work can advance.\012- Set Status: COMPLETE only when every original gate and its required in-scope\012  follow-up/child checkboxes are implemented and verified. New children must neither\012  broaden original scope nor hide unfinished original acceptance criteria.\012  Otherwise use Status: IN_PROGRESS when material progress permits continuation.\012  Maintain exactly one of these status lines in the tracking file opening header, after an\012  optional # title and before the first ## (or deeper) section heading, outside\012  code fences. Historical status lines belong in sections below the header.\012\012When Runner context says Recovery verification required: YES, re-evaluate the\012persisted terminal status and acceptance evidence before trusting prior completion.\012The last session may have failed, timed out or exhausted quota after editing state.\012Preserve proven work and flags, validate remaining criteria, then continue ready tasks.\012\012\012Runner context:\012Runner role: WORKER\012Source roadmap snapshot: "/Users/bpstr/Github/assign/.roadmap-runner/astro-web-rewrite-194449dbe604/.roadmap-runner/astro-web-rewrite-194449dbe604/source-456a9fd2739e.md"\012Source roadmap revision: 8c52e86230cc4451ce02ffbcd8184a58f43de4b8502de53204699108b1c4bee5\012Roadmap reconciliation required: NO\012Use this read-only snapshot for all source requirements in this session, not the changing live file.\012An external controller may revise the live roadmap; adopt later revisions only on the next run.\012Neither workers nor the periodic supervisor may edit the live source